            "resource_file_links": [],
            "evaluations": [
              {
                "id": "130b8fcf-41c9-40fe-af6d-0f7e11e2276d",
                "objective_id": "c8b77d76-ce8d-4aac-aca3-85cac4dc5714",
                "evaluation_type": "ai",
                "order": 0,
                "points": 50,
                "data": {
                  "model": "gpt-4o",
                  "platform": "open_ai",
                  "max_tokens": 1000,
                  "temperature": "0.1",
                  "user_prompt": "<p><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;\">`Grading Criteria`</span></p><h3><br></h3><p><strong>Clarity and Relevance of Thesis (10 points): </strong>Evaluate the extent to which the essay presents a clear, arguable, and specific thesis related to <em>To Kill a Mockingbird</em>. The thesis must focus on a particular aspect of the novel, such as a theme, character development, moral conflict, or symbolism.</p><ul><li><strong>10 points:</strong> Thesis is clear, specific, and directly addresses a significant aspect of the novel with a well-defined argument.</li><li><strong>5 points:</strong> Thesis is somewhat clear but lacks specificity or relevance to a critical element of the novel.</li><li><strong>0 points:</strong> Thesis is unclear, overly general, or not directly related to <em>To Kill a Mockingbird</em>.</li></ul><p><br></p><p><strong>Depth of Literary Analysis (10 points): </strong>Assess how well the essay examines key literary elements such as themes, motifs, character arcs, or the setting of <em>To Kill a Mockingbird</em>. Look for evidence of critical thinking and insight into the text.</p><ul><li><strong>10 points:</strong> Analysis demonstrates a deep understanding of the novel, providing original insights and nuanced interpretations of its literary elements.</li><li><strong>5 points:</strong> Analysis shows some understanding of the text but lacks depth or originality.</li><li><strong>0 points:</strong> Analysis is superficial, overly simplistic, or misinterprets the novel.</li></ul><p><br></p><p><strong>Use of Evidence from the Text (10 points): </strong>Determine the quality and integration of textual evidence used to support the essay’s thesis and analysis. Quotes and references should be relevant, accurately cited, and effectively woven into the argument.</p><ul><li><strong>10 points:</strong> Evidence is consistently relevant, well-chosen, and seamlessly integrated to support the thesis and analysis.</li><li><strong>5 points:</strong> Evidence is occasionally relevant but may lack variety, proper integration, or sufficient connection to the argument.</li><li><strong>0 points:</strong> Evidence is absent, irrelevant, or misinterpreted.</li></ul><p><br></p><p><strong>Exploration of Social and Historical Context (10 points): </strong>Evaluate the extent to which the essay incorporates the social and historical context of the 1930s American South as it relates to <em>To Kill a Mockingbird</em>. Consider how the context is used to enhance the analysis.</p><ul><li><strong>10 points:</strong> Context is skillfully integrated and enhances the understanding of the novel’s themes, conflicts, or characters.</li><li><strong>5 points:</strong> Context is mentioned but not effectively integrated into the analysis or lacks depth.</li><li><strong>0 points:</strong> Context is absent, irrelevant, or misrepresented.</li></ul><p><br></p><p><strong>Character and Theme Interconnection (10 points): </strong>Assess how well the essay connects character development to overarching themes in the novel, such as racism, justice, or moral growth. The connections should be clear and well-supported.</p><ul><li><strong>10 points:</strong> Essay insightfully explores the relationship between character arcs and themes, providing well-supported connections that enhance the argument.</li><li><strong>5 points:</strong> Essay makes some connections between characters and themes but lacks depth or clarity.</li><li><strong>0 points:</strong> Essay fails to connect character development to the novel’s themes or does so incorrectly.</li></ul><p><br></p>",
                  "system_prompt": "<ol><li><span style=\"background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255); color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">You are an expert educator assisting a classroom teacher. Your task is to review student-submitted files, evaluate them based on specific `Grading Criteria`, provide formative feedback to the student, and offer a critical assessment to the classroom teacher.</span></li></ol>"
                },
